Interface variables are public static final by default
    interface Vehicle {
        int wheels = 4;
    }

Compiler treats it as:

    interface Vehicle {
        public static final int wheels = 4;
    }



The constant belongs to the contract and can be accessed publicly.

System.out.println(Vehicle.wheels);

The variable belongs to the interface itself, not to individual implementing objects.

Vehicle.wheels

Interface variables represent constants, not changeable instance data.

Vehicle.wheels = 6; // Error

Because final means it cannot be reassigned.

An interface traditionally contains:

interface Example {

    // Method → public abstract
    void display();

    // Variable → public static final
    int VALUE = 10;
}

Interface = Contract + Constants

Methods → What implementing classes must do
Variables → Constants shared by everyone

Important modern Java exception

Since Java 8, interfaces can also contain:

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("Default implementation");
    }

and

    static void test() {
        System.out.println("Static method");
    }

Since Java 9, they can also contain private methods.

So the more accurate statement is:

    Interface methods without a body are implicitly public abstract, and interface fields are implicitly public static final.


In a normal class :

    class Animal {
        void eat() {
        }
    }

Here, no access modifier means default (package-private).

In an interface :

    interface Animal {
        void eat();
    }

Java language rules say that a method declared in an interface without a body is implicitly: public abstract void eat();